# XSH tmpfile()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.

assertion  XSH.tmpfile.001
anchor     functions/tmpfile.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/tmpfile.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       tmpfile() returns a non-null stream on a regular file of size 0
           with ftell() 0; after fwrite() of the five bytes 'a', 'b', 0x00,
           0xff, '\n', fflush() and rewind(), fread() returns the same five
           bytes; after fseek(f, 0, SEEK_SET), fputc('Z') and fseek(f, 0,
           SEEK_SET), getc() returns 'Z' and then 'b'.
test       t/001-update-stream.c

assertion  XSH.tmpfile.002
anchor     functions/tmpfile.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       After tmpfile(), fputs("hello"), fflush() and dup() of the
           stream's descriptor, fclose() of the stream leaves the data
           readable: pread() of 5 bytes at offset 0 from the duplicate
           returns "hello".
note       The deletion itself, when the last reference is closed, is not
           checked: the file's name is not known to the caller.
test       t/002-reference-keeps-file.c

assertion  XSH.tmpfile.003
anchor     functions/tmpfile.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       With umask 0 the permission bits of the file are 0666, 0600 or 0;
           with umask 022 they are 0644, 0600 or 0.
note       The page lets an implementation restrict the permissions by
           clearing the bits or setting them to read and write for the owner;
           anything else is the fopen() mode for "wb+" (0666 less the umask).
test       t/003-permissions.c

assertion  XSH.tmpfile.004
anchor     functions/tmpfile.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/tmpfile.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       Two tmpfile() calls return two streams on different files
           (different st_dev or st_ino); after "one" is written to the first
           and "two" to the second, each reads back only its own text.
test       t/004-distinct-files.c

assertion  XSH.tmpfile.005
anchor     functions/tmpfile.html#ERRORS/EMFILE
anchor     functions/tmpfile.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       When every descriptor below the process's descriptor limit is
           open, tmpfile() returns a null pointer and sets errno to EMFILE.
note       The only applicable error with every descriptor open; the page
           lists EMFILE as one that shall be reported. The data of the failed
           call is not looked at (XSH 2.3).
test       t/005-emfile.c
